The '''University of Michigan''' ('''UMich''', '''U-M''', or '''Michigan''') is a [[public university|public]] [[research university]] in [[Ann Arbor, Michigan]], United States. It was founded in 1817 and is the oldest institution of higher education in the state. It is also one of the earliest American research universities and is a founding member of the [[Association of American Universities]].

The university has the largest student population in Michigan, enrolling more than 53,000 students, including more than 35,000 undergraduates and 18,000 postgraduates. It is classified as an "R1: Doctoral Universities – Very high research activity" by the [[Carnegie Classification of Institutions of Higher Education|Carnegie Classification]].{{sfn|Gilman|2017|p=102}} It consists of 19 schools and colleges, offering more than 280 degree programs.{{sfn|Desprez|Pehlke|Zichi|2025}} The university is [[Higher education accreditation in the United States|accredited]] by the [[Higher Learning Commission]]. In 2021, it ranked third among American universities in [[List of countries by research and development spending|research expenditures]] according to the [[National Science Foundation]].

The campus spans {{convert|3,177|acre|km2|sp=us}}. It encompasses [[Michigan Stadium]], which is the [[list of U.S. stadiums by capacity|largest stadium in the United States]], as well as the [[Western Hemisphere]], and ranks [[List of stadiums by capacity|third globally]]. The University of Michigan's athletic teams, including 13 men's teams and 14 women's teams competing in intercollegiate sports, are collectively known as the [[Michigan Wolverines|Wolverines]]. They compete in [[NCAA Division I]] ([[NCAA Division I Football Bowl Subdivision|FBS]]) as a member of the [[Big Ten Conference]]. Between 1900 and 2022, athletes from the university earned a total of 185 medals at the [[List of American universities with Olympic medals|Olympic Games]], including 86 gold.

==History==
{{Main|History of the University of Michigan}}

===Origins===

The proposal for establishing an [[institution of higher education]] in Michigan dates back to 1703, during the colonial period of [[New France]].{{sfn|Hinsdale|1906|p=6}}{{sfn|Sheldon|1856|p=112}}{{efn|The ''séminaires'' in the colonies were primarily institutional transplants from France. In terms of their spirit and mission to prepare individuals for the priesthood, these institutions closely resembled their counterparts in France, which were often advanced ecclesiastical establishments. The training involved rigorous academic formation, including the defense of theses. However, the realities of a frontier society meant that they could not simply exist as carbon copies of those in France. In their early years, in an attempt by Jean-Baptiste Colbert, King Louis XIV's minister, to impose the French language and culture on the colonies, the ''séminaires'' in New France mainly served as preparatory schools for Indigenous people as well as for children of settlers with studious dispositions and a desire to enter the priesthood. They would only later evolve into true ''séminaires'' when circumstances allowed.{{sfn|Magnuson|1992|pp=3–15}}}} Just two years after founding [[Fort Pontchartrain du Détroit]] on the strait between Lakes [[Lake Saint Clair|Saint Clair]] and [[Lake Erie|Erie]] in 1701, the French explorer and later colonial governor of [[La Louisiane|Louisiana]], [[Antoine de la Mothe Cadillac]], wrote to King [[Louis XIV]]'s minister, [[Louis Phélypeaux de Pontchartrain]], from the settlement under the date of August 31, 1703.{{sfn|Sheldon|1856|p=112}} He urged the establishment of a seminary in the newly formed parish of [[Basilica of Sainte Anne de Détroit|Sainte-Anne-de-Détroit]] for the education of both Indigenous and French children in piety and the French language.{{sfn|Sheldon|1856|p=112}}{{sfn|Hinsdale|1906|p=6}}{{sfn|Ross|1898|p=79}} It remains uncertain whether a seminary was ever established;{{sfn|Farmer|1889|p=720}} the surviving ''registres'' make no mention of a seminary being created from the proposal.{{sfn|Fuller|Catlin|1928|pp=119–120}} [[Jesuit]] superiors in [[Quebec City|Quebec]] probably opposed the idea of creating a seminary in the ''[[Pays d'en Haut]]'', due to disputes with Cadillac and fears of rivalry with their own institutions.{{sfn|Sheldon|1856|p=137}} Parish records from 1755 indicate, nevertheless, that Jean Baptiste Rocoux was "Director of Christian Schools",{{sfn|Farmer|1889|p=720}} a title likely influenced by [[Jean-Baptiste de La Salle]].{{sfn|Hinsdale|1906|p=6}} He kept a school either at his residence on St. Jacques Street or in a building under the patronage of Ste. Anne's Church.{{sfn|Fuller|Catlin|1928|p=120}} Mentions are made of a seminary of unknown founding at the fort that trained young men for the ministry,{{sfn|Poor|Griffin|1959|p=22}}{{sfn|Judson|1926|pp=268–288}} but it was destroyed in the [[Great Fire of 1805]].{{sfn|Poor|Griffin|1959|p=22}} Additionally, there was a French [[subscription school]] that stood near the fort in 1775, and an old account book preserved from 1780 contains tuition charges dated as early as 1760.{{sfn|Hinsdale|1906|p=6}}

The colony was surrendered to the British monarchy in 1762 following the [[French and Indian War]]. The local [[French Canadians]] maintained the Christian schools, which became bilingual;{{sfn|Mantz|1976|p=18}}{{sfn|Hinsdale|1906|p=7}} however, the British, who viewed the colony merely as a trading post, did practically nothing for education, leading to stagnation during their rule from 1763 to 1796.{{sfn|Hinsdale|1906|p=7}}{{sfn|Burton|Stocking|Miller|1922|pp=700–721}}

When the colony came under American control following the enforcement of the [[Treaty of Paris (1783)|1783 Treaty of Paris]] in 1796, the territorial judges were supposed to be called in to formally define the rights and legal status of the Christian schools under the new constitution. Thereafter, the interpretation of the American right to education in Michigan, grounded in Section 1, Article XI of the [[Northwest Ordinance]], was laid out.{{sfn|Shearman|1852|p=1}} Since 1806, parish-minister [[Gabriel Richard]], who presided over the Christian schools and helped plan a new layout for Detroit after the [[Great Fire of 1805|fire in 1805]],{{sfn|Poor|Griffin|1959|pp=21–22}} petitioned for land to found an "institution for higher learning" on several occasions{{sfn|Burton|Stocking|Miller|1922|pp=723–726}}{{sfn|Hinsdale|1906|p=7}} and suggested that a lottery might be used to support the academies he headed.{{sfn|Baisnée|1952|pp=269-270}}{{sfn|Dunbar|May|1995|p=188}} Subsequently, in 1817, during the postwar period following the [[War of 1812]], the [[Territory of Michigan|Territorial government]], at the instigation of Fr. Richard{{sfn|Shaw|1942|pp=14–15}}{{sfn|Hinsdale|1906|p=7}}{{sfn|Burton|Stocking|Miller|1922|p=723}} and Judge [[Augustus B. Woodward]]{{sfn|Shaw|1942|pp=14–15}}{{sfn|Shaw|1920|p=8}}{{sfn|Burton|Stocking|Miller|1922|p=723}} and with the support of U.S. President [[Thomas Jefferson]],{{sfn|Shaw|1942|pp=14–15}} passed "an Act to establish the '''Catholepistemiad''', or '''University of Michigania'''" within the [[Territory of Michigan]].{{sfn|Shearman|1852|pp=4–5}} Enacted on August 26, 1817,{{sfn|Farmer|1889|p=728}}{{sfn|Bersey|1890|p=26}} the Act effectively consolidated the schools into one institution. Rev. [[John Monteith (minister)|John Monteith]], a [[Presbyterian]] minister, was named as its first president{{sfn|Poor|Griffin|1959|pp=22–24}} and Fr. Richard as vice president.{{sfn|Poor|Griffin|1959|pp=21–22}} Its ''didactors'' had authority not only over the university itself but also over education in the territory in general.{{sfn|Poor|Griffin|1959|p=22}}{{sfn|Hinsdale|1906|pp=8–10}} The legislative act was signed into law by Acting Governor and Secretary [[William Woodbridge]], Chief Judge Augustus B. Woodward, and Judge [[John Griffin (judge)|John Griffin]].{{sfn|McLaughlin|1891|pp=29–31}}

=== The Catholepistemiad, 1817–1821 ===

{{multiple image
| direction = vertical
| align     = right
| width     = 250
| header    = 
| image1    = STEANNESCHURCH1818.jpg
| caption1  = 
| image2    = Catholepistemiad Detroit Sainte-Anne 1818.png
| caption2  = From top to bottom: Ste. Anne's "Stone Church", built in 1818; The Catholepistemiad's schoolhouse (center) on an 1818 map of Detroit. The schoolhouse fronts Bates Street, directly across from Ste. Anne's "Stone Church" (middle of the lower right).
}}

The university was modeled after an institution established in France a decade earlier, known as the ''[[University of France|Université imperial]]'', under Emperor [[Napoleon]].{{sfn|Hinsdale|1906|p=10}}{{sfn|Shaw|1920|pp=7–22}} The term "Catholepistemiad", a neologism derived from a blend of Greek and Latin roots, can be loosely translated as "School of Universal Knowledge".{{sfn|Dunbar|May|1995|p=189}} This entity included an array of schools and libraries under a single administration,{{sfn|Hinsdale|1906|pp=8–9}} with the authority to establish additional schools across the territory.{{sfn|Hinsdale|1906|p=10}}{{sfn|Shaw|1920|pp=7–22}} It was not until the legislative council passed the territory's first public school law on April 12, 1827, which made basic education a municipal duty, that the corporation focused solely on higher education.

The university may have initially been funded through private donations and federal land grants.{{sfn|Castilla|Hodgeson|2017|pp=6–23}} Shortly after its founding in 1817, it received a $250 subscription from the [[Zion Lodge No. 1 F&AM|Freemason Zion Lodge of Detroit]].{{sfn|Castilla|Hodgeson|2017|pp=6–23}} Of the total amount subscribed to establish the university, two-thirds came from the Zion Lodge and its members.{{sfn|Castilla|Hodgeson|2017|pp=6–23}}{{sfn|Hinsdale|1906|p=12}} Early benefactors included [[Joseph Campau]] (1769–1863) and his nephew [[John R. Williams]] (1782–1854). The first endowment may have been a land grant from the U.S. federal government on May 20, 1826, as part of "an Act concerning a seminary of learning in the territory of Michigan," based on the [[Treaty of Fort Meigs|Treaty at the Foot of the Rapids]] and the [[Treaty of Detroit|Treaty at Detroit]].{{sfn|Castilla|Hodgeson|2017|pp=6–23}}{{sfn|Burton|Stocking|Miller|1922|p=757}}

The cornerstone of the University Building, located on Bates Street directly across from the 1818 "Stone Church" of [[Basilica of Sainte Anne de Détroit|Ste. Anne Parish]] in Detroit,{{sfn|Palmer|Hunt|June|1906|pp=674–709}}{{sfn|Poor|Griffin|1959|p=23}} was laid on September 24, 1817.{{sfn|Hinsdale|1906|p=12}} By the following year, a [[Lancasterian school]], taught by [[Lemuel Shattuck]],{{sfn|Poor|Griffin|1959|p=22}} and a classical academy, taught by Hugh M. Dickey,{{sfn|Poor|Griffin|1959|p=23}} were in operation.{{sfn|Farmer|1889|pp=728–732}}{{sfn|Hinsdale|1906|p=12}} The [[Detroit Public Library|city library of Detroit]], incorporated on August 26, 1817, was initially located in the University Building, with university teachers serving as librarians.{{sfn|Farmer|1889|p=710}} Over the years, the University Building was occupied by various teachers,{{sfn|Farmer|1889|pp=715–727}} including D. B. Crane, who taught Latin and Greek and established a chemistry laboratory in the building.{{sfn|Palmer|Hunt|June|1906|pp=674–709}} The University Building later served as the Detroit Branch School after the university moved to Ann Arbor.{{sfn|Farmer|1889|pp=728–732}}

[[File:First Annual Report University of Michigania.JPG|thumb|left|First Annual Report of the ''University of Michigania'', authored on November 16, 1818]]

In 1821, by a new enactment, the university itself was created as a "body politic and corporate",{{sfn|Shaw|1942|p=117}} maintaining its corporate status through various modifications to its charter.{{sfn|Hinsdale|1906|p=11}} The new act placed the corporation under the control of a board of trustees.{{sfn|Hinsdale|1906|pp=12–13}} Rev. Monteith joined the board shortly but left the university for New York a year later, despite being offered the chairmanship.{{sfn|Poor|Griffin|1959|pp=22–24}} The trustees continued to manage the schools and classical academy, but established no new schools.{{sfn|Hinsdale|1906|p=14}} By 1827, the first public school law mandated that local governments organize their own school districts within each township.{{sfn|Burton|Stocking|Miller|1922|pp=730–731}} Thereafter, the university leased the Detroit schoolhouse to private teachers,{{sfn|Hinsdale|1906|p=14}} and the first school in Detroit under this law opened about the first June by an order of the trustees.{{sfn|Burton|Stocking|Miller|1922|pp=730–731}} However, the school soon closed following the passing of its teacher.{{sfn|Burton|Stocking|Miller|1922|pp=730–731}} The following year, the [[Bridge School (Michigan)|Bridge School]] in [[Raisinville Township]] was established under local governance.

===Early years in Ann Arbor, 1837–1851===

[[File:Library and Chapel, University of Michigan, Ann Arbor (front elevation) MET DT369502.jpg|thumb|[[Alexander J. Davis]]'s original designs for the university featured the Gothic Revival style. He is generally credited with coining the term "[[Collegiate Gothic]]"]]

Following Michigan's admission to the Union in 1837, an organic act was passed on March 18, 1837, to reorganize the university under a twelve-member board of regents.{{sfn|Shaw|1942|p=32}} The regents met in [[Ann Arbor]] and accepted the town's proposal for the university to relocate to Judge [[Henry Rumsey]]'s farmland.{{sfn|Shaw|1942|p=32}}

The approved campus plans for the university were drawn up by the architect [[Alexander Jackson Davis|Alexander Davis]].{{sfn|Truettner|2003|pp=1,9}}{{sfn|Mayer|2015|p=}} Davis designed an elaborate [[Gothic architecture|Gothic]] main building with a large lawn in front, wide avenues, and botanical gardens, all arranged to evoke the French ''[[château]]'' aesthetic.{{sfn|Truettner|2003|pp=1,9}} He also provided possible sites for future buildings; however, the plans were never executed. Instead, four houses for professors were authorized. Historians attribute the abandonment of the original plan to the financial constraints the university faced as a result of the [[Panic of 1837]].{{sfn|Hinsdale|1906|p=31}} Construction began in 1839, and in 1841, [[The Diag#Former buildings|Mason Hall]], the first campus building, was completed, followed by the construction of [[The Diag#Former buildings|South College]], a nearly identical building to the south, in 1849, leaving a gap for a future grand centerpiece.

[[File:Colored elevation of Mason Hall.jpg|thumb|A colored elevation of [[The Diag#Former buildings|Mason Hall]] (1841–1950), the first instructional building on the Ann Arbor campus. Its design inspired [[North Hall (University of Wisconsin)|North Hall]] (1851) in [[Madison, Wisconsin]]]]

The first classes in Ann Arbor were held in 1841, with six freshmen and one sophomore taught by two professors, Joseph Whiting and George Palmer Williams. [[Asa Gray]] was the first professor appointed following the university's move to Ann Arbor in 1837.{{sfn|Dupree|1988|pp=67–68}} He and the regents were both involved in stocking the [[University of Michigan Library|university library]].{{sfn|Palmer|Hunt|June|1906|p=857}} In 1846, Louis Fasquelle, a native of France, was appointed as the first professor of modern languages, primarily teaching French and writing textbooks.{{sfn|Duderstadt|2006|p=}} French became the first modern language taught at the university. During the first commencement in 1845, eleven graduates, including [[Judson Dwight Collins|Judson Collins]], were awarded Bachelor of Arts degrees.{{sfn|Wiley|1858|p=122}}

After the university moved to Ann Arbor, the regents created several branches across the state,{{sfn|Russel|1934|p=}} with the Detroit Branch School being the most significant. Advocated by R. C. Gibson in 1837, it opened in the renovated University Building on Bates Street in 1838,{{sfn|Catlin|1923|p=227}} initially serving boys with one principal and one assistant.{{sfn|Farmer|1889|pp=728–732}} The school operated four terms a year, with its first public examination held in 1838.{{sfn|Farmer|1889|pp=728–732}} Many students were parishioners of Ste. Anne Church, and notable alumni included [[Anson Burlingame]] and [[E. C. Walker]].{{sfn|Palmer|Hunt|June|1906|pp=674–709}} Other branches were established in cities like [[Pontiac, Michigan|Pontiac]], [[Kalamazoo, Michigan|Kalamazoo]], [[Niles, Michigan|Niles]], and [[Tecumseh, Michigan|Tecumseh]], but struggled with enrollment, leading some to merge with local colleges, such as [[Kalamazoo College]], which was affiliated with the University of Michigan from 1840 to 1850.{{sfn|Russel|1934|p=}}

The administration during the early years of the university was complicated and designed to keep it tightly under state authority.{{sfn|Moos|Rourke|1959|pp=17–42}} The university's business was often intertwined with state affairs.{{sfn|Moos|Rourke|1959|pp=17–42}} The position of chancellor of the university, created by the organic act in 1837,{{sfn|Hinsdale|1906|p=26}} was never filled, and the positions on the board of regents, appointed by the governor, were often held by state officials. The [[Lieutenant Governor of Michigan|lieutenant governor]], the justices of the [[Michigan Supreme Court]], and the chancellor of the state all served as ex officio members of the board, with the governor himself chairing the board. The regents' powers were shared with a rotating roster of professors, who were responsible for some vague aspects of the university's administrative duties; however, all important decisions had to be made by the governor and his party.{{sfn|Hinsdale|1906|pp=17,40}} There were several attempts to gain independence from the state legislature, but progress was slow until the late 1840s, when the regents gained leverage, supported by Michigan citizens.{{sfn|Hinsdale|1906|p=40}} This shift culminated in a revision of the organic act on April 8, 1851, which freed the university from legislative control, transitioned the regent positions from appointed to elected, and established a president selected by the regents.{{sfn|Moos|Rourke|1959|pp=17–42}}{{sfn|Hinsdale|1906|p=40}}

===1851–1900===

[[File:JasperCropseyUniversityOfMichigan.jpg|thumb|''University of Michigan'' (1855) by [[Jasper Francis Cropsey]]]]

[[Henry Philip Tappan]] became the university's first president in 1852, with the ambition to shape the institution as a model for future universities. During his decade of service, he overhauled the curriculum,{{sfn|Shaw|1942|pp=39–53}} expanded the library and museum collections,{{sfn|Shaw|1942|pp=39–53}} established the law school, and supervised the construction of the [[Detroit Observatory]].{{sfn|Shaw|1942|pp=39–53}} He secularized faculty appointments by prioritizing merit in selections,{{sfn|Shaw|1942|pp=39–53}} breaking away from the retrograde tradition of regents distributing positions among Protestant denominations.{{sfn|Shaw|1942|pp=39–53}} In 1855, Michigan became the second university in the country to issue [[Bachelor of Science]] degrees.{{sfn|Hinsdale|1906|p=48}} The following year, the country's first chemical laboratory was built on campus, specifically designed for chemistry education, providing additional space for classes and laboratories. Tappan's tenure also saw the creation of the [[University of Michigan Men's Glee Club|Michigan Glee Club]], the oldest student organization at the university, and the publication of the first student newspaper, ''The Peninsular Phoenix and Gazetteer'', in 1857.{{sfn|Humphrey|1898|pp=136–139}} Despite these accomplishments, Tappan's 11-year presidency was marked by considerable tension.{{sfn|Shaw|1942|pp=39–53}} His impartial stance on religion faced backlash during a time of heightened religious fervor.{{sfn|Shaw|1942|pp=39–53}} Due to changes in the Board of Regents and discontent with his administration, he was forced to resign in 1863.{{sfn|Shaw|1942|pp=39–53}}

[[File:Mason Hall 1841.jpg|thumb|left|Mason Hall, with a statue of [[Benjamin Franklin]] in front. Photograph taken prior to 1870.]]

In 1863, [[Erastus Otis Haven]] took office as president, having been a professor at the time and needing to prove his right for the presidency.{{sfn|Shaw|1942|pp=54–59}} The campus was divided by conflicting views among students, faculty, and regents regarding Tappan's restoration, the homeopathy crisis, and the [[American Civil War|Civil War]].{{sfn|Shaw|1942|pp=54–59}} Haven's administration faced routine administrative difficulties and struggled to garner support for increased state aid, despite achieving modest gains.{{sfn|Shaw|1942|pp=54–59}} The university, which had received a fixed $15,000 since 1869, still required additional funding.{{sfn|Shaw|1942|pp=54–59}} Frustrated, Haven resigned in 1869 to become president of [[Northwestern University|Northwestern]], a [[Methodist]] institution, a move that sectarians viewed as a setback for secular colleges.{{sfn|Shaw|1942|pp=54–59}} The presidency remained vacant from 1869 to 1871, with Professor [[Henry Simmons Frieze]] serving as acting president.{{sfn|Shaw|1942|pp=59–62}} During this period, the university raised funds for University Hall, overhauled admissions with a diploma system, and introduced coeducation.{{sfn|Shaw|1942|pp=59–62}} Women were first admitted in 1870,{{sfn|Burmester|2006|pp=6–25}}{{sfn|Stockwell|1988|p=}} although [[Alice Robinson Boise Wood]] was the first woman to attend classes in 1866.{{sfn|Huber|1970|p=}}{{sfn|Bordin|2001|p=}} In 1871, [[Sarah Killgore]] became the first woman to graduate from law school and be admitted to the bar of any state in the United States.{{sfn|Willard|Livermore|1893|p=759}} Frieze, a champion of music education, also established the [[University Musical Society]].{{sfn|Shaw|1942|pp=59–62}} By the late 1860s, the university had become one of the largest in the nation, alongside Harvard in [[Cambridge, Massachusetts|Cambridge]]. However, it faced ongoing issues with student discipline, including class rushes, instances of hazing, and rowdiness in chapel.{{sfn|Shaw|1942|pp=59–62}} Frieze attributed these problems to a lack of centralized faculty control.{{sfn|Shaw|1942|pp=59–62}}

[[File:Bhl BL004525 BL004525 bl004525.jpg|thumb|[[The Diag#Former buildings|University Hall]] (center, 1872–1950), flanked by [[The Diag#Former buildings|Mason Hall]] (left, 1841–1950) and [[The Diag#Former buildings|South College]] (right, 1849–1950), was located partly on the grounds of present-day [[Angell Hall]].]]

[[James Burrill Angell]] became president in 1871 and would remain in the post for nearly four decades.{{sfn|Shaw|1942|pp=63–75}} His tenure would be remembered as the most successful in the university's history.{{sfn|Shaw|1942|pp=63–75}} Tappan's reforms in the 1850s set the university on a path to becoming an elite institution, but it was Angell who completed that transformation.{{sfn|Shaw|1942|pp=63–75}} Shortly after Angell's arrival, [[The Diag#Former buildings|University Hall]] was completed at vast expense; it would remain the university's major academic building right up until the 1950s.{{sfn|Shaw|1942|pp=63–75}} During his presidency, Angell restored campus discipline,{{sfn|Shaw|1942|pp=63–75}} raised entrance and graduation requirements,{{sfn|Shaw|1942|pp=63–75}} and persuaded the legislature to increase state aid.{{sfn|Shaw|1942|pp=63–75}} Angell's tenure saw the addition of many extracurricular activities, including the [[Michigan Wolverines football|intercollegiate football team]].{{sfn|Shaw|1942|pp=63–75}} Though a reformer, Angell was not authoritarian; he encouraged open debate and aimed for near-unanimous agreement before implementing changes, rather than pushing through with only a narrow majority.{{sfn|Shaw|1942|pp=63–75}} This approach enabled him to address knotty issues on campus, including the long-standing homeopathy problem.{{sfn|Shaw|1942|pp=63–75}} Angell transformed the curriculum to focus on electives, expanding course offerings.{{sfn|Shaw|1942|pp=63–75}} That led to a faculty of great minds in many fields, from [[John Dewey]] in philosophy to [[Frederick George Novy]] in bacteriology.{{sfn|Miller|1973|pp=xii–xix}} In 1875, the university founded the [[University of Michigan School of Dentistry|College of Dental Surgery]],{{sfn|Vedder|Bunting|Ward|1925|p=16}} followed by the establishment of the [[University of Michigan College of Pharmacy|College of Pharmacy]] by [[Albert B. Prescott]] in 1876. That year, the university awarded its first [[Doctor of Philosophy]] degrees: to [[Victor C. Vaughan]] in chemistry and William E. Smith in zoology.{{sfn|Vaughan|1926|pp=76–97}}{{sfn|Hinsdale|1906|p=}} They were among the first doctoral degrees to be conferred in the nation.{{sfn|Vaughan|1926|pp=76–97}}

